---
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout title="GitHub Actions Self-Hosted Runner Guide">
  <main class="prose mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1>Using a GitHub Actions Self-Hosted Runner</h1>
    <p>This guide explains how to set up and use a self-hosted runner for your project to avoid GitHub-hosted runner costs.</p>
    <h2>1. Register Your Runner</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Go to <b>Settings → Actions → Runners</b> in your GitHub repository.</li>
      <li>Click <b>"New self-hosted runner"</b> and follow the instructions for your OS.</li>
      <li>When prompted, you can add a custom label (e.g. <code>Linux-Ubuntu</code>).</li>
      <li>Start the runner with <code>./run.sh</code> in the <code>actions-runner</code> directory.</li>
    </ol>
    <h2>2. Workflow Configuration</h2>
    <p>Your workflow file should use your custom label:</p>
    <pre><code>runs-on: [self-hosted, Linux-Ubuntu]</code></pre>
    <p>Example workflow:</p>
    <pre><code>{`name: Build Locally

on:
  workflow_dispatch:

jobs:
  build:
    runs-on: [self-hosted, Linux-Ubuntu]
    steps:
      - name: Checkout
        uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - name: Detect package manager
        id: detect-package-manager
        run: |
          if [ -f "\${{ github.workspace }}/yarn.lock" ]; then
            echo "manager=yarn" >> $GITHUB_OUTPUT
            echo "command=install" >> $GITHUB_OUTPUT
            echo "runner=yarn" >> $GITHUB_OUTPUT
            echo "lockfile=yarn.lock" >> $GITHUB_OUTPUT
            exit 0
          elif [ -f "\${{ github.workspace }}/package.json" ]; then
            echo "manager=npm" >> $GITHUB_OUTPUT
            echo "command=ci" >> $GITHUB_OUTPUT
            echo "runner=npx --no-install" >> $GITHUB_OUTPUT
            echo "lockfile=package-lock.json" >> $GITHUB_OUTPUT
            exit 0
          else
            echo "Unable to determine package manager"
            exit 1
          fi
      - name: Setup Node
        uses: actions/setup-node@v4
        with:
          node-version: "20"
          cache: \${{ steps.detect-package-manager.outputs.manager }}
          cache-dependency-path: \${{ steps.detect-package-manager.outputs.lockfile }}
      - name: Install dependencies
        run: \${{ steps.detect-package-manager.outputs.manager }} \${{ steps.detect-package-manager.outputs.command }}
      - name: Install marked
        run: npm install marked --save
      - name: Build with Astro
        run: \${{ steps.detect-package-manager.outputs.runner }} astro build
`}</code></pre>
    <h2>3. Running the Workflow</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Start your runner with <code>./run.sh</code> (leave this terminal open).</li>
      <li>Go to the <b>Actions</b> tab in your repo and click <b>Run workflow</b> for <b>Build Locally</b>.</li>
      <li>The workflow will run on your local/server machine, not on GitHub-hosted infrastructure.</li>
    </ol>
    <h2>4. Tips</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>You can add more custom labels to target specific runners.</li>
      <li>Monitor your runner status in <b>Settings → Actions → Runners</b>.</li>
      <li>Stop the runner with <code>Ctrl+C</code> when not in use.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>5. Why Use a Self-Hosted Runner?</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>No GitHub-hosted runner costs</b> for private repos or heavy usage.</li>
      <li>Full control over the build environment.</li>
      <li>Can use your own hardware or cloud VM.</li>
    </ul>
  </main>
</Layout>
